This issue has already occurred in #4737 and #4774.

Extracted from #4551:

The reason for the occasional fails of `LocalFeedUpdaterTest` is probably the concurrency caused by `dbExec.submit()` calls in `DBWriter`. I found two different approaches that solved the problem on my computer (no fails in more than 20 test executions):

**1) Modify `DBWriter`**

Most methods with return type `Future<?>` are called without using the return value. After I changed the return type to `void` and removed the `dbExec.submit()` call the problem disappeared.

Drawback: Android doesn't like too much work handled in UI threads. This could lead to warnings in the app at runtime.

**2) Modify `LocalFeedUpdaterTest`**

Alternatively, I could solve the problem by adding the following lines to the method `LocalFeedUpdaterTest.setUp()`. It's a hack that overwrites the private field `DBWriter.dbExec` by a direct executor to avoid using different threads.

```
   public void setUp() throws Exception {
        // Don't fork processes in DBWriter
        ExecutorService directExecutorService = new AbstractExecutorService() {

            @Override
            public void execute(Runnable command) {
                command.run();
            }

            @Override
            public void shutdown() {
            }

            @Override
            public List<Runnable> shutdownNow() {
                return null;
            }

            @Override
            public boolean isShutdown() {
                return false;
            }

            @Override
            public boolean isTerminated() {
                return false;
            }

            @Override
            public boolean awaitTermination(long timeout, TimeUnit unit) {
                return false;
            }
        };
        Field field = DBWriter.class.getDeclaredField("dbExec");
        field.setAccessible(true);
        @SuppressWarnings("JavaReflectionMemberAccess")
        Field modifiersField = Field.class.getDeclaredField("modifiers");
        modifiersField.setAccessible(true);
        modifiersField.setInt(field, field.getModifiers() & ~Modifier.FINAL);
        field.set(null, directExecutorService);

        ... (everything else)
```

Do you feel comfortable with one of the two approaches? If yes, I could provide an appropriate pull request.
Hmm, both sound rather hacky. Is the problem that it is asynchronous within one single test or that calls "survive" one test and are then executed during the next one? In that case, something like `dbExec.shutdown();` could maybe help.
After hours of debugging I finally could find the cause of the occasional Robolectric test fails:

When running more than one unit test method in `LocalFeedUpdaterTest` the asynchronous methods

* `DBWriter.addDownloadStatus()` and
* `DBWriter.setFeedLastUpdateFailed()`

(_asynchronous_, because they use `dbExec.submit()` to run in a `ThreadPoolExecutor`) may run delayed and open a new database connection (via `PodDBAdapter.getInstance()`) even after `LocalFeedUpdaterTest.tearDown()` has finished.

As Robolectric provides an individual thread context for each unit test method (with a separate temporary SQLite database file) a thread with an old context (previous unit test method) opens a new database connection that is reused in a new thread context (next unit test method). This is detected by Robolectric and leads to the "Illegal connection pointer" error.

Pull request #4783 provides a fix for that, it waits for the two asynchronous methods to be finished before the test continues.

Generally, we should be careful with asynchronous methods as they can lead to strange race conditions. I can imagine that some of the open issues in the AntennaPod project that cannot be reproduced are caused by concurrency.
Wouldn't it be more generic to call `dbExec.shutdown()` after the test? It then covers all asynchronous calls to DbWriter at once. We can not make all calls synchronous because this slows down feed parsing or blocks the UI thread. So for other tests, we would need something like that, anyway.

